RaiseError called directly in a :CATCH handler re-raises past the handler
the code just entered: raiseerror_in_catch (warning) on the RaiseError
identifier. The RaiseError in the :TRY body is the blessed placement and
must not flag (diag.raiseerror_in_catch).
-- input.ssl --
:PROCEDURE ValidateOrder;
	:PARAMETERS nQty;
	:DECLARE oErr;
	:TRY;
		:IF nQty <= 0;
			RaiseError("Quantity must be positive");
		:ENDIF;
	:CATCH;
		oErr := GetLastSSLError();
		RaiseError("ValidateOrder failed: " + oErr:Description);
	:ENDTRY;
	:RETURN .T.;
:ENDPROC;
-- format --
:PROCEDURE ValidateOrder;
	:PARAMETERS nQty;
	:DECLARE oErr;

	:TRY;
		:IF nQty <= 0;
			RaiseError("Quantity must be positive");
		:ENDIF;
	:CATCH;
		oErr := GetLastSSLError();
		RaiseError("ValidateOrder failed: " + oErr:Description);
	:ENDTRY;

	:RETURN .T.;
:ENDPROC;
-- diagnostics --
10:3-10:13 warning raiseerror_in_catch: 'RaiseError' inside ':CATCH' - the error handler should not raise; move the raise into the ':TRY' block it belongs to
